{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And the tablets were the work of Elohim, etc.</b> This whole verse has no place here but rather above, in the verse ״וַיִּתֵּן אֶל מֹשֶׁה כְּכַלֹּתוֹ וְגוֹ׳ לֻחֹת אֶבֶן כְּתֻבִים בְּאֶצְבַּע אֱלֹהִים״, and there it would have been fitting to mention the nature of the tablets. And it appears to me to say that this whole verse is to inform us that the letters were חָרוּת upon the tablets, for he wished thereby to give a reason why Moses broke the tablets. Rather, it is because the tablets were מַעֲשֵׂה אֱלֹהִים and the writing was מִכְתַּב אֱלֹהִים — it was חָרוּת upon the tablets. And they said in the Midrash (Exodus Rabbah 41:7): חָרוּת — freedom from the kingdoms, and others say freedom from the angel of death, and the Rabbis say freedom from suffering. And they derived all this from the fact that it is written ״חָרוּת עַל הַלֻּחֹת״ and it does not say ״חָקוּק״ or ״כָּתוּב״, but rather there was a tangible reality in those letters, in the manner that we explained in parashat Yitro on the verse ״וְכָל הָעָם רֹאִים אֶת הַקּוֹלֹת״, that every utterance that went forth from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, was embodied and had tangibility so that they were able to see it, and those utterances were engraved upon the tablets. It does not say ״בַּלֻּחֹת״ but ״עַל הַלֻּחֹת״ — they were fixed upon them from above. Therefore they expounded ״חָרוּת״ in the sense of חֵרוּת, to say: just as those letters were free men and were not enslaved to the tablets at all and were not annulled when the tablets were annulled — for behold, when the tablets were broken the letters flew away — if so, they are certainly not enslaved to the materiality of the stone. So too all who learn Torah will likewise be free men from the kingdoms, from suffering, and from the angel of death. And therefore it says (Exodus 34:1) ״וְכָתַבְתִּי עַל הַלֻּחֹת אֶת הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר הָיוּ עַל הַלֻּחֹת הָרִאשֹׁנִים״ — it does not say ״כַּדְּבָרִים״ but ״אֶת הַדְּבָרִים״, to tell you that those very letters that flew from the first tablets would be fixed here. And since this matter teaches that all the while the tablets endure, Israel has a carved covenant that they shall be free from the kingdoms, and now that they have sinned they are not worthy of all these promises, therefore he broke them. As it is written (Psalms 82:6) ״אֲנִי אָמַרְתִּי אֱלֹהִים אַתֶּם״ — that you would endure as children of Elohim — ״חִבַּלְתֶּם מַעֲשֵׂיכֶם״, ״אָכֵן כְּאָדָם תְּמוּתוּן״ — for you shall no longer be free from the angel of death." "And the reason Moses did not break the tablets immediately when HaShem told him how they had made the calf — it is because he infers in the Yalkut (Exodus 32, remez 893) that on account of the letters flying away the stones became heavy and fell from Moses' hand — and according to this we may say that the letters were already flying away beneath the mountain when they saw the calf, and then Moses sensed that the sanctity had been withdrawn from them; therefore he broke them beneath the mountain specifically." "And there are those who say that he broke them because they were like a deed of debt against Israel, and a torn deed of debt leaves the claimant with nothing by which to demand of them — so too these tablets were testimony against Israel that they had received the Torah." "And there are those who say that he broke them in order to join even himself with them in the sin: Israel caused the letters to fly away, and Moses broke the tablets — so that the Holy One, blessed be He, would not be able to say, 'I will consume them and make of you a great nation,' for behold they were equal in the sin. And concerning this Moses said ״וְעַתָּה אִם תִּשָּׂא חַטָּאתָם״, meaning: and now let a new matter be renewed — that I too am a sinner like them; and if so, if You will bear their sin, well and good, and if not, then You will not bear mine either, and if so — ״מְחֵנִי נָא מִסִּפְרְךָ״."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:25:05.490062913Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}